# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-release-binaries-lose-exe — the Windows release shipped unrunnable names, a Linux binary, and images built from another commit
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: alfe-sbcl-windows-build-missing-exe-suffix
#+BRANCH: disable-arm32-release

* Symptom

pjb, 2026-08-21: « les executables dans
libexec/clautolisp/binaries/windows/x86-64/ n'ont pas l'extension .exe ;
revoir le makefile. »

Confirmed on the PUBLISHED release-1.9.0 asset rather than on a local
build — downloaded =clautolisp-1.9.0-binaries-windows-x86-64.zip= from
the release and listed it:

#+begin_example
 13608760  libexec/clautolisp/binaries/windows/x86-64/alfe-sbcl
 57023592  libexec/clautolisp/binaries/windows/x86-64/clautolisp-sbcl
 49158336  libexec/clautolisp/binaries/windows/x86-64/read-autolisp-sbcl
#+end_example

* Why this is worse than a naming quibble

*Both shipped launchers open =<prog>-<lisp>.exe=.* They are the only
supported way to start these programs, and they are in the same archive.

=clautolisp/tools/packaging/dispatch.cmd=:

: set "_BIN=%~dp0..\libexec\clautolisp\binaries\windows\%_A%\%~n0-%_L%.exe"
: if not exist "%_BIN%" (
:   echo %~n0: no binary for windows/%_A% with lisp=%_L% 1>&2
:   exit /b 127 )

=clautolisp/tools/packaging/dispatch.sh=:

: bin="$prefix/libexec/clautolisp/binaries/$os/$arch/$prog-$lisp"
: [ "$os" = windows ] && bin="$bin.exe"   # Windows executables keep .exe

So a user who installs release-1.9.0 on Windows and runs =clautolisp= is
told « no binary for windows/x86-64 with lisp=sbcl » and gets exit 127.
*The Windows products of release-1.9.0 do not start.* The archive is
42 MB, perfectly well-formed, and complete apart from three names.

* Cause — an MSYS2 courtesy turning the fallback into dead code

=release-programs= copied each binary with:

: if [ -f "$b" ]; then cp "$b" "$bindir"/; \
: elif [ -f "$b.exe" ]; then cp "$b.exe" "$bindir"/; fi;

which reads as "take the bare one, else the .exe" — correct-looking, and
wrong on Windows in a way that cannot be seen by reading it on Linux.

MSYS2's POSIX layer *opens =foo= when only =foo.exe= exists*. So
=[ -f "$b" ]= is TRUE for a file that is only present as =.exe=, the
first branch always wins, and =cp "$b" "$bindir"/= names the destination
after the path it was given — without the extension. The =elif= exists
precisely for Windows and never runs there.

The evidence is in the artefact itself: the copied files have the sizes
of the real =.exe= binaries, so the source WAS the =.exe=; only the
destination name lost it.

* Fixed

Two changes in =release-programs=, both load-bearing:

1. *test =.exe= FIRST.* Under MSYS2 the bare test is ambiguous and the
   =.exe= test is not, so the unambiguous one has to be asked first.
2. *name the destination explicitly* (=cp "$b.exe" "$bindir/$n.exe"=)
   rather than copying into a directory. Then it does not matter what
   name the source resolved through.

* And the check that should have caught it

=make verify-release-artefacts= is what every release lane runs to assert
it produced something. It only checked that the file exists and is over
10 000 bytes — so a 42 MB archive whose contents no launcher can open
passed, which is exactly the "looks complete and is not" failure this
repository keeps meeting.

It now OPENS the binaries artefact and requires the entries the launcher
will actually reach for: =<prog>-<lisp>.exe= on Windows,
=<prog>-<lisp>= elsewhere. It reads the ARCHIVE, not the staging
directory, because what ships is what was packed and the defect happened
during packing.

*Verified in three directions*, on this Linux box, by building the
artefacts by hand (=REL_OS=windows= is overridable, which is what makes
the Windows branch testable without the machine):

| fixture                                    | result |
|--------------------------------------------+--------|
| the shape release-1.9.0 actually shipped   | FAILS, naming what it holds instead and what that costs |
| the same with =.exe=                         | passes |
| POSIX =tar.bz2= with bare names              | passes — no regression |

A check nobody has watched fail proves nothing, which is why the first
row exists.

* Two worse defects behind the same symptom

pjb looked further into the same archive and found two things that the
missing =.exe= was only the surface of. Both are established from the job
log of =release:windows:x86-64= (16026668736), not inferred.

** =alfe-sbcl= is a LINUX ELF, not a Windows program

: $ file libexec/clautolisp/binaries/windows/x86-64/alfe-sbcl
: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, ... for GNU/Linux 3.2.0, stripped

The other two are genuine =PE32+ executable for MS Windows=. Only alfe is
foreign.

*Cause*: =.release-common= declared no =needs:=, so GitLab's default
applied — a job with no =needs:= receives the artifacts of EVERY job in
every earlier stage. The log shows it plainly:

: Downloading artifacts for build:alfe:sbcl (16026668717)...

and =build:alfe:sbcl= is a LINUX docker job publishing exactly

: autolisp-front-end/tools/alfe/bin/alfe-sbcl

So the Linux ELF was written into the Windows workspace at that path. The
Windows build then produced =alfe-sbcl.exe= BESIDE it, and the packaging
loop — testing the bare name first — took the ELF.

macOS escaped only by luck: there the built name IS the bare name, so the
build overwrote the download instead of sitting next to it.

*Fixed*: =dependencies: []= on =.release-common=. A release lane builds
from source and must inherit nothing. =dependencies= rather than =needs:
[]= on purpose — =needs= would also detach the job from stage ordering and
let a release start before the tests run.

** The images were dumped from ANOTHER COMMIT's fasls

pjb: « clautolisp-1.9.0-all.zip contient un clautolisp version 1.8.56 » and
« read-autolisp est une version 1.8.0 ». Both are versions from days before
the tag, inside an archive named 1.9.0.

*The tell is the DURATION*: the whole image build took *three seconds*.
With the cache cleared, the same build on the same machine takes *twenty
minutes* (release-1.9.1, job 16032119087). Three seconds cannot compile
those systems; it can only dump an image from fasls that were already
there.

I first wrote that the absence of =; compiling= lines was the tell. *That
is wrong, and worth correcting rather than quietly dropping*: the 1.9.1
build compiles for twenty minutes and prints no such line either — ASDF
is silent at this verbosity. Anyone re-reading the original claim would
have been given a test that cannot distinguish the two cases. Only the
duration does.

*Cause*: =XDG_CACHE_HOME= points at =clautolisp/.cache=, which lives
INSIDE the workspace and is gitignored — and the Windows runner cleans
with =-ffd=, not =-ffdx=, so ignored files survive between pipelines. That
policy exists for a good reason (a held-open file under =dist/= used to
kill every Windows job, see =.windows-clean=), and its own comment even
warns that "a release quietly built on top of a previous run's leftovers
is worse than one that fails". The consequence for the FASL CACHE had not
been drawn: surviving fasls mean the release image is compiled from an
older checkout.

This is the worst failure this lane can have — a binary that is not its
own source — and it is completely silent: green job, well-formed archive,
correct file names. Only running the program tells you.

*Fixed*: =scripts/release-windows.sh= now clears the fasl cache before
building, the same way and for the same reason it already clears =dist/=,
and refuses to build if it cannot. It costs a cold compile on the release
lane, which is where correctness beats speed.

** And the check that sees it

Names and sizes cannot detect a stale image. =verify-release-artefacts=
therefore also *runs each binary* and requires =--version= to report the
version being released. Verified both ways with stand-in binaries:

| fixture                                        | result |
|------------------------------------------------+--------|
| reporting 1.8.56 / 1.8.0 (the 1.9.0 shape)     | FAILS, quoting what each one reports |
| all three reporting the released version       | passes |

* Consequences for release-1.9.0, to decide

The published release cannot be repaired in place. Its Windows asset
holds the wrong names, and =make-gitlab-release.py --update= only rewrites
LINKS — it cannot change what a pipeline already packed. Repairing it
means a new tag whose pipeline packs correctly, i.e. *release-1.9.1*.

The other platforms name their binaries correctly (no =.exe= is expected
there) and build on runners that clean with =-ffdx=, so their fasl caches
do not survive — but *that has not been verified by running them*, and
after three defects found by looking inside one archive, "unaffected"
should not be asserted from reasoning alone. The version check added here
is what will answer it on the next tag, for every platform at once.

* Note

Found because pjb looked inside the archive. Nothing in the pipeline did
— which is the point of the check added here.
